u have your 5 models then comes your 5th and most important step that you will run your own evaluation on it on those 5 models and those 5 models when your own evaluation set will run and the results that they will give, then you will get your top candidate so here one line that you have to remember this is the most important line that you have to take from this session that leaderboards are not a selection tool leaderboards are a filtering tool not a decision tool, meaning you don't go to leaderboard and decide that I will use this model to make my application, no that is a wrong strategy you shortlist 4 to 5 top models with the help of leaderboards and then you run c\", \"stion which model provides the strongest overall combination of capability, cost and performance this is the leaderboard that you will use the most third is another interesting category this is called human preference based leaderboards in this you don't make a rank based on a benchmark what you do is you make a comparison so your user comes to your website you ask him a question same question is asked by two models A and B both the answers are there now you ask the user whether you liked A or B so basically you are scoring yourself on the basis of helpfulness, clarity, writing quality, creativity and the way of giving the answer and when a lot of these types of votes are collected then a ranking is made on the basis of that and that ranking is shown in this leaderboard here it is written\", \"tep one is model selection and in model selection also step one is to go to the leaderboards and see which model is at the top so we get the bias that if it is on the leaderboard then it will be right but it is not, you cannot blindly trust leaderboards what are the reasons we are discussing the first reason is that benchmark performance may not transfer to real applications this is very important if a model is hitting 80-90 it does not mean that it will give you the same results in your real application like the famous thing that if you solve the problem on Kaggle it does not mean that you will become a good data scientist in real life why was it said like that? because the data that is given on Kaggle is very clean problem statement is very clear generally you get easy work, everything i\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Contextual Recall", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "evaluationCost": 0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": false}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Contextual Precision", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "evaluationCost": 0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": false}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"(generator not evaluated in this run)\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": \"An evaluation pipeline can be executed in one of three ways, and the distinction is simply about who is carrying out the eval. The first is programmatic, also called deterministic\\u2014a program or code executes the evaluation. The second is human\\u2014a human carries out the eval. The third is model-graded, also called LLM-graded\\u2014an LLM executes the evaluation. Any eval pipeline you build will be run by one of these three: a program, a human, or an LLM, and there's nothing beyond these three. For example, in the Zomato email-classification system, the accuracy was calculated through Python code, so that was a programmatic eval.\", \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"what are the different ways an eval can actually be run?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\". Basically, evals are different for Latency and all types of metrics. In the last video, we discussed what are the different eval methods through which we perform evaluations. We discussed three things in this video. Programmatic methods, LLMEvalse-based as a judge, and how to perform eval with humans. In nutshell, this is the summary of whatever we have covered in this course till now. In today's video, we are going to discuss a very important topic. The topic is Offline Eval vs Online Eval. First, I will tell you what is offline eval, then I will tell you what is online eval, and then what are the differences between them. We will see that and you will learn a lot in this process. This is our agenda for today. This is the topic that we are going to cover today\", \". This is our agenda for today. This is the topic that we are going to cover today. Is it clear till now? What have we studied till now and what are we going to study today? Is the agenda clear? Write Y for yes so that we are on the same page. Easy, right? If you are attending all the classes, then you must be understanding what we are doing. Let's first discuss what offline evals are. The good thing is that you don't have to read anything new here. You have already read offline evals. All the examples that we have discussed till now in the last 2-3 sessions where I have made an eval pipeline for you, all those eval pipelines are an example of offline eval\", \"is is what I showed you how step by step Evaluation is performed So these three things we have covered so far in the previous lecture And today's lecture will start from exactly this point So at this point things are clear So quickly say thumbs up or yes so that we can start the session Just tell me if you are on the same page It's not like there is a gap Okay So I will start the session from the last line of the previous session So in the previous session we were reading So after showing the whole Evaluation Pipeline I told you this last line I told you that generally speaking one LLM based application have several LLM evals I told you that when you make any LLM application there is not just one single Eval Pipeline Okay With this statement we will start our session today Okay So first of\", \"main focus of this course will be application evals here you will learn about the evaluation of the rag and agent after that we discussed an eval pipeline basically how evals work then we discussed an interesting point why do we need multiple eval pipelines and in the last video we covered an important topic of online evals that once you do the evaluation of your application you deploy it then how to evaluate it even after deployment we discussed this in the last video so in a way I can say that we have discussed an overview of this course now we will go into specifics we will cover what we have studied in detail like the main goal of today's video is that we will cover model evals model evals means evals which you use to test LLM capabilities so we will start working on model evals from\", \"will be called eval generator.py in which we will write the code to evaluate it similarly you can understand a rag pipeline will have a different file an application will have a different file to test a different file for safety a different file for operations so basically all these files in the eval folder where we have written all our tests we call this our eval suite and then what will happen outside all the folders we will have a file which we will call run evals.py and what code will be written in this as soon as we trigger this file then what will this file do one by one all these tests will be run on our application and will generate a report and will give it and when you analyze that report then you will understand that your new version of the software is objectively better than th\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Contextual Recall", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "evaluationCost": 0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "l