 called human preference based leaderboards in this you don't make a rank based on a benchmark what you do is you make a comparison so your user comes to your website you ask him a question same question is asked by two models A and B both the answers are there now you ask the user whether you liked A or B so basically you are scoring yourself on the basis of helpfulness, clarity, writing quality, creativity and the way of giving the answer and when a lot of these types of votes are collected then a ranking is made on the basis of that and that ranking is shown in this leaderboard here it is written\", \"tep one is model selection and in model selection also step one is to go to the leaderboards and see which model is at the top so we get the bias that if it is on the leaderboard then it will be right but it is not, you cannot blindly trust leaderboards what are the reasons we are discussing the first reason is that benchmark performance may not transfer to real applications this is very important if a model is hitting 80-90 it does not mean that it will give you the same results in your real application like the famous thing that if you solve the problem on Kaggle it does not mean that you will become a good data scientist in real life why was it said like that? because the data that is given on Kaggle is very clean problem statement is very clear generally you get easy work, everything i\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Contextual Recall", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "evaluationCost": 0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": false}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Contextual Precision", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "evaluationCost": 0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": false}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"(generator not evaluated in this run)\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": \"The RAG triad is a set of three metrics used to evaluate a RAG pipeline, based on the three things present after a query runs: the question, the retrieved context, and the generated answer. Context relevance checks whether the retrieved context is relevant to the question, faithfulness checks whether the answer is grounded in the retrieved context, and answer relevance checks whether the answer actually addresses the question.\", \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"What is the RAG triad?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"by the way I think on the basis of today's session you can kind of tackle this question don't you think? do you think if someone asks you this question now how do you evaluate your rag chatbot will you be able to answer this question? think about it, it's very simple what you have to say is first I will build an evaluation suite that person will ask what is an evaluation suite you will say that I will test my application at three levels I will test it on the component level where I will test the retriever and generator tell me the name of the metrics then I will test it at the pipeline level tell me rag triad then I will test it at the application level correctness, completeness then I will test the safety and operation metrics and once this whole thing is done I will use all of these and\", \"hat I have thought for you that we will make a rag application and we will make it at this level the whole page that you can see on the screen we will test it according to this so my plan of action is that in the next class I will teach you the complete component level evaluation which basically means that in the next class I will make you a retriever and then teach you to test the retriever then I will make a generator and then I will teach you to test the 