 between answer and question and the metric for this we call answer relevance here basically it is told that given a question the answer generated is relevant for the question or not so we in isolation test these three metrics of our rag pipeline if these three metrics are working properly it means that your rag pipeline is working properly and if these three metrics are done then our particular level will be done so so far the flow is clear to everyone, what we are doing we are not doing that we have made our rag chart board first and then we are evaluating it no, this is not how it works your building the chart board and evaluating it it works together, it is just like software software we don't do that that you make the whole application and\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Contextual Recall", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "evaluationCost": 0.0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Contextual Precision", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "evaluationCost": 0.0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "llama-3.3-70b-versatile", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"(generator not evaluated in this run)\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": \"Faithfulness, which is sometimes also called groundedness, means that the answer was generated on the basis of the given context\\u2014the relevant retrieved documents\\u2014and that no extra facts were invented beyond what the context contained. For example, if someone asks the duration of the machine learning course and the retrieved document says 3 weeks, the answer should state that the duration is 3 weeks and nothing more. It should not add unrelated extra information, like saying it's a great course or pitching the Python course. The answer must stay grounded in the context.\", \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"faithfulness vs groundedness kya hota hai?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"are captured values whatever is coming, we are storing it as it is coming but we will also capture some things like when our software is deployed online how is its faithfulness or how is its answer relevance or how is its correctness the things we tested offline the same things we test online so this is also our work after that we check drift drift basically means that over time our software performance is not getting bad I had told this in a lecture that we have a last 24 hours graph in that we are tracking some metric for example faithfulness so suddenly in the last 8 hours the curve of faithfulness is going down so this is a kind of drift which we are detecting and with that we do alerting and after alerting we improve it so you have to do this whole thing once the software is deployed\", \"hat are they same or not So first of all you will test That are their numbers of rows Same or not Think and tell me If the number of rows of two tables are different Then can they be same tables In a sequel result set What do you think Guys If you get two tables You searched Who is the top 5 batsmen You can see 5 rows in one And 3 rows in the other So obviously the 3 row query was wrong So first you will check on the row After that You will check After that What will you do First of all the values in both the tables You will kind of Normalize them Normalize means not the normalize Which I told you earlier Not the min max Normalize means Like There is decimal value at one place 2.0 and integer value at one place So at one place 2.0 is there And at the same place 2 is there So you will treat\", \"peline an evaluation pipeline which will check that given a query are you getting the right relevant documents You will set up this pip